__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
# Every environment file, not only the bare `.env`: that pattern matched that exact
# name and nothing else, so `COPY . .` carried `.env.production` — and the secret key
# in it — into the image. The container takes its environment from the compose
# `env_file`, which is read on the host, so no copy inside the image was ever used.
.env*
.venv
*.log
*.pid
*.swp
*.swo
media/
static/
node_modules/
.git/
# Per-machine assistant state; the tracked one was a symlink into a home directory.
.antigravitycli/
# The database and everything kept beside it. `*.sqlite3` reads as if it covered these, and
# does not: a `.dockerignore` pattern's `*` never crosses a `/`, so it matches `db.sqlite3`
# at the root and nothing inside `db/` — where the file and its 20 MB timestamped copies
# actually live. The volume supplies the real one at runtime.
db/
backups/
*.backup.*
# The local production replica's TLS material. `mojon.local.key` is a private key, tracked
# nowhere and generated per machine, and it sat two directories away from `COPY . .`.
.docker/
# Per-machine tooling and editor state, none of it the application.
.claude/
.idea/
.vscode/
.mcp.json
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
TODO.md
# What GitHub runs, not what the container does.
.github/
# External channel lists and captures: other people's data, and never part of a release.
# `addlinksource` reads them from a mount, which is the only place they belong.
*.m3u
newera.txt
elcano.txt
screenshot*.png
